Make the following targeted visual and navigation improvements to Deployment Manager. Do not remove, rename, or alter any existing functionality or data other than the specific UI changes described below.

1. REPLACE "CONTAINERS 2" WITH A "SIDECAR" BADGE

In the main Deployment Manager deployment list, currently a multi-container workload is displayed like:

Pods 2/2 Ready    Containers 2

This is potentially confusing to non-technical users because "Containers 2" can be interpreted as two Pods.

Replace the container-count badge with a simple:

Sidecar

badge/chip.

Example:

Pods 2/2 Ready    [Sidecar]

or:

Pods 1/1 Ready    [Sidecar]

The Sidecar badge should use the existing badge/chip visual language used elsewhere in the application.

Do not display "Containers 2".

Do not display the number of sidecars.

Do not create another line for the badge.

Keep it on the same line as the Pods readiness information.

Only show the Sidecar badge when the workload actually contains an applicable sidecar container.

For normal single-container deployments, do not show the Sidecar badge at all.

Important:
The Pods count must continue to represent actual Pod/replica count.

For example:

Pods 2/2 Ready    [Sidecar]

means there are two Pods, and the workload uses a sidecar.

Do not interpret the sidecar as another replica.

2. PODS SECTION: EXPAND / COLLAPSE

The Pods section on the Deployment Manager detail page currently displays the Pod cards vertically.

Add an expand/collapse control in the top-right corner of the Pods section.

Default state:
EXPANDED

When expanded:
- Show all discovered Pods.
- Preserve the existing Pod card design and all existing information.
- Users should be able to see all Pods without manually expanding the section first.

When collapsed:
- Show only the Pods section header and the compact header information for the Pod list.
- Do not render the full Pod detail/resource cards.
- The user can click the arrow again to expand.

Use a small, subtle chevron/arrow control in the top-right corner.

Do not introduce a large button or additional text.

3. PODS SECTION INTERNAL SCROLLING

If the number of Pods becomes large enough that the Pods section exceeds the available browser/page viewport, constrain the scrolling to the Pods section itself.

Do NOT allow the entire page layout to become unnecessarily stretched because of a large number of Pods.

The Pods panel should become vertically scrollable when its content exceeds the available display area.

The scroll should apply ONLY to the Pods list/content area.

The Deployment Overview, Conditions, Rollout History, Recent Events, and other surrounding sections should remain in their existing layout.

For example, if a Deployment has 5, 6, 10, or more Pods, the user should be able to scroll through the Pods inside the Pods panel without the entire application layout becoming unusable.

4. SCROLLBAR BEHAVIOR

Use the application's existing shared/custom scrollbar implementation if one already exists.

Do not introduce a new scrollbar style if a reusable shared implementation already exists.

The scrollbar should:
- Remain visually minimal
- Be hidden/subtle when not being interacted with
- Become visible when the mouse is moved over the Pods scrolling area or when the user is actively scrolling
- Match the existing application's modern dark/neon visual language
- Not permanently occupy unnecessary visual space

Do not show a scrollbar when the Pods content fits completely within the available area.

5. POD CARD COLLAPSED HEADER

When the Pods section itself is collapsed, only retain the compact Pod header-level information that is useful for identifying the Pods.

The collapsed representation should include the existing key information such as:
- Pod name
- Overall Running/Ready status
- Timestamp/age
- Node
- Ready state

Do not show the full CPU/memory cards, image details, health badges, or other detailed container information while the Pods section is collapsed.

Do not remove that information from the expanded state.

6. NOW RUNNING WIDGET / ACTIVE JOB NAVIGATION

There is an existing "Now Running" widget that displays the currently running Deployment Manager job.

When a Deployment Manager operation such as Restart, Stop, Start, or Scale is running, the operation already has an action/progress view that uses the application's WebSocket/pipeline-style execution UI.

Currently, clicking the Now Running widget can incorrectly navigate to/open the older terminal-style execution view instead of returning to the existing Deployment Manager action/progress view.

Investigate this end-to-end and fix the routing/state handling.

Expected behavior:

If a Deployment Manager action is currently running and the user navigates away from the Deployment Manager page:

1. The running execution must continue normally in the backend.
2. The execution state must remain associated with the existing Deployment Manager action/run.
3. Clicking the Now Running widget must return the user to the SAME Deployment Manager action/progress view that was originally used for that execution.
4. It must NOT open the legacy terminal view.
5. It must NOT start another execution.
6. It must NOT create a duplicate job.
7. It must NOT lose the existing WebSocket/action execution state.

Trace the existing execution/run ID from:
Deployment Manager action
→ job execution
→ Now Running widget
→ navigation target
→ action/progress view

Identify why the Now Running widget is resolving to the terminal execution route instead of the Deployment Manager action/progress route.

Fix the routing so the Now Running widget resolves to the correct execution UI based on the job/action type.

Do not simply hardcode the Deployment Manager route. Use the existing execution metadata/type/action information so that the navigation remains correct and maintainable.

7. IMPORTANT: PRESERVE EXISTING EXECUTION FLOW

Do not replace the current WebSocket/pipeline-style Deployment Manager action screen.

That existing action screen is the intended UI for showing operations such as:
- Restart
- Stop
- Start
- Scale

The Now Running widget should navigate back to that existing screen when the corresponding Deployment Manager execution is active.

The terminal should only be used by execution types that genuinely require the terminal-style interface.

8. REGRESSION CHECKS

After implementing the changes, verify:

- Single-container deployment shows no Sidecar badge.
- Multi-container/sidecar deployment shows [Sidecar].
- Sidecar badge appears on the same line as Pods readiness.
- Pod replica counts remain correct.
- 1 Pod with a sidecar still displays Pods 1/1, not 2/2.
- Multiple Pods with sidecars still display the correct Pod replica count.
- Pods section is expanded by default.
- Pods section can be collapsed and expanded.
- Large Pod lists scroll inside the Pods section only.
- Scrollbar is hidden/subtle when inactive and visible during interaction.
- No scrollbar appears when unnecessary.
- Collapsing the Pods section hides detailed Pod cards while retaining the compact header.
- Existing Pod information remains intact when expanded.
- Clicking Now Running for an active Deployment Manager operation returns to the existing Deployment Manager action/progress view.
- No terminal window is opened for the Deployment Manager action.
- Existing WebSocket progress/status behavior continues to work.
- No duplicate execution is created.
- Existing jobs and unrelated execution types continue using their current navigation behavior.

Keep the current visual design, spacing, animations, typography, colors, cards, and overall Deployment Manager layout. Make these changes as focused improvements rather than redesigning unrelated areas.
